1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
My dad was an engineer, so he was a beaver about the proper hierarchy of the world.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
I was tempted to say, well, what should be done for this engineer?

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Should there be a program to give sub-slave houses to engineers?

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,000
They're like, what are you talking about?

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
I know he said, yeah, you should let people build more houses.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:19,000
No, you should let there be more traffic.

7
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Well, what do you want from me?

8
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Anyway, even smart people lose their intelligence, they lose lots of IQ points when they deliberate on political and non-questions.

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,000
They approach them in this very religious way.

10
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,000
And so I say voter rationality is two things.

11
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:46,000
It's plausible in theory because the cost is so low, and when we go and just do very simple tests, we can see huge amounts of it.

12
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,000
So, looking pretty good.

13
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Now, Whitman likes to go and offer a challenge to critics, and he says, tell me the asymmetry between politics and markets.

14
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Why aren't they basically just two different ways that people get what they want?

15
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,000
With similar efficiency properties.

16
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Why would it be that shopping for policies is worse than shopping for groceries?

17
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:15,000
And my answer there is there's a big asymmetry, which is incentives for rationality.

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
So in markets, people at least try to use some common sense.

19
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,000
They don't know everything, in fact they often know very little, but at least there's common sense.

20
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Normally.

21
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
And not forgetting, but for other stuff.

22
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:34,000
So I'm not saying common sense is always operative, but in general, consumers have common sense, and I do play the game when I go to the grocery store,

23
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,000
of just eavesdropping on people talking.

24
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Even people who I think would do terribly on economics exam,

25
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,000
they're very subtle comparisons, saying this one looks better, but you get more per ounce.

26
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,000
If you only look at it like that, you only look at the more expensive, but you get more per ounce this way, so actually it's cheaper.

27
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Or saying yes, this one is ten cents more per ounce, but the quality is much better and it lasts longer.

28
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Comparisons that are so much more thoughtful than anything they would ever make for any government policy.

29
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,000
I mean, I can't imagine a person like this saying, look, the Iraq war was better for Iraq, but worse for Syria.

30
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:11,000
You never hear an old person say, make a distinction that fine or subtle.

31
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,000
You might say, that's not that fine or subtle. Exactly.

32
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,000
But just to get to that level, of saying, look, three out of the seven affected countries are worse off.

33
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:23,000
I think that's a good deal, because those are lower population countries.

34
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,000
What kind of person would say such a thing?

35
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,000
And yet it's a totally reasonable point to make.

36
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:34,000
So anyway, markets ignorant actors are still at least trying to do the best with what they've got.

37
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
In politics, they scarcely try.

38
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,000
So in markets, if someone says, I have a great idea, I can't really explain it to you, but give me all your money,

39
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:44,000
and you'll find out how great it is after I get your money.

40
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,000
You will say, no, no money.

41
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
In politics, if someone says, hey, I want a trillion dollars, I'm going to fix the health care system, it's going to be great, trust me.

42
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
You'll find out how it works later.

43
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Okay, yeah, sounds like a good deal.

44
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,000
No, it doesn't.

45
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:04,000
I refuse, that does not sound like the kind of pitch that would win over a reasonable person.

46
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,000
What, do you want children to die?

47
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,000
No, I guess I have to do whatever you say, because I don't want children to die.

48
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Your plan.

49
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:20,000
And then I go on and say, this rational rationality story explains why, contrary to Whitman,

50
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
you can't go and fix society by mailing your articles to politicians.

51
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
You know what happens when they get it.

52
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,000
Say, will this help me keep and gain power?

53
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,000
No, throw it away, who needs it?

54
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
What's the point?

55
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Rather, politicians cater their prejudices, they don't try to educate them,

56
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:45,000
and voters like candidates who share their confusions, they don't want patents for real life.

57
